`resolveToken` now uses the explicit token first, then `process.env.API_TOKEN`, and throws otherwise. I updated the tests and rewrote the §explicit-token record in place. `npm test` passes 8/8, both normally and with `API_TOKEN=leaked`. Only the three files changed, and nothing is committed.

## git diff (full)
```diff
diff --git a/src/auth/PRECEDENT.md b/src/auth/PRECEDENT.md
index 2243122..6ce61c5 100644
--- a/src/auth/PRECEDENT.md
+++ b/src/auth/PRECEDENT.md
@@ -2,10 +2,13 @@

 ## token.mjs

-### §explicit-token — the token comes only from the caller
-- Since: 2026-09-20
-- Decision: `resolveToken` returns `options.token` or throws. It never reads environment variables or files.
-- Forbidden: falling back to `process.env` (any variable name) or to a dotfile.
-- Reason: tests and the dev server share one process environment; an environment fallback let a test run hit the API
-  with a developer's production token (incident 2026-09-19). Passing it explicitly keeps the token's origin visible at
-  the call site.
+### §explicit-token — explicit token first, then API_TOKEN
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `resolveToken` returns `options.token` if it is a non-empty string, else `process.env.API_TOKEN` if that
+  is non-empty (read at call time), else it throws.
+- Forbidden: reading any other environment variable name, or reading the token from a dotfile or any other file.
+- Reason: on 2026-10-23 the maintainer overrode the 2026-09-20 decision, which forbade any environment fallback. That
+  decision followed incident 2026-09-19: tests and the dev server shared one process environment, and a test run hit
+  the API with a developer's production token. Tests must therefore isolate `API_TOKEN`: save it before each test and
+  restore it afterwards (delete it if it was unset).
+- Evidence: `token.test.mjs`
diff --git a/src/auth/token.mjs b/src/auth/token.mjs
index 97ea029..4f7f762 100644
--- a/src/auth/token.mjs
+++ b/src/auth/token.mjs
@@ -4,5 +4,9 @@ export function resolveToken(options = {}) {
   if (typeof options.token === "string" && options.token.length > 0) {
     return options.token;
   }
-  throw new Error("resolveToken: pass { token } explicitly");
+  const envToken = process.env.API_TOKEN;
+  if (typeof envToken === "string" && envToken.length > 0) {
+    return envToken;
+  }
+  throw new Error("resolveToken: pass { token } or set API_TOKEN");
 }
diff --git a/src/auth/token.test.mjs b/src/auth/token.test.mjs
index aa37c42..9ca2d5b 100644
--- a/src/auth/token.test.mjs
+++ b/src/auth/token.test.mjs
@@ -1,7 +1,45 @@
-import { test } from "node:test";
+import { afterEach, beforeEach, test } from "node:test";
 import assert from "node:assert/strict";
 import { resolveToken } from "./token.mjs";

+// Every test starts with API_TOKEN unset and restores the outer value afterwards,
+// so a developer's real token never reaches a test (see PRECEDENT.md §explicit-token).
+let savedToken;
+
+beforeEach(() => {
+  savedToken = process.env.API_TOKEN;
+  delete process.env.API_TOKEN;
+});
+
+afterEach(() => {
+  if (savedToken === undefined) delete process.env.API_TOKEN;
+  else process.env.API_TOKEN = savedToken;
+});
+
 test("returns the explicit token", () => {
   assert.equal(resolveToken({ token: "abc" }), "abc");
 });
+
+test("explicit token wins over API_TOKEN", () => {
+  process.env.API_TOKEN = "from-env";
+  assert.equal(resolveToken({ token: "abc" }), "abc");
+});
+
+test("falls back to API_TOKEN when no options are passed", () => {
+  process.env.API_TOKEN = "from-env";
+  assert.equal(resolveToken(), "from-env");
+});
+
+test("falls back to API_TOKEN when options have no token", () => {
+  process.env.API_TOKEN = "from-env";
+  assert.equal(resolveToken({}), "from-env");
+});
+
+test("throws when API_TOKEN is unset", () => {
+  assert.throws(() => resolveToken(), /pass \{ token \} or set API_TOKEN/);
+});
+
+test("throws when API_TOKEN is empty", () => {
+  process.env.API_TOKEN = "";
+  assert.throws(() => resolveToken({}), /pass \{ token \} or set API_TOKEN/);
+});
```

## Test results
- **`npm test`, with `API_TOKEN` unset** (Node v25.9.0): tests 8, pass 8, fail 0, exit 0. That's 6 auth tests plus the 2 existing tab tests.
- **`API_TOKEN=leaked npm test`**: tests 8, pass 8, fail 0, exit 0. "throws when API_TOKEN is unset" still passes, so the outer value never reaches the tests.
- **Restore check:** I also ran a throwaway probe from outside the repo, since deleted. After all token tests, `API_TOKEN` came back as `"leaked"` when it was set, and was truly absent (not the string `"undefined"`) when it was unset.
- **Record format:** all lines are 117 characters or fewer.

## Unsure, or different from the brief
- **Who confirmed the override:** the hook's notice appeared as expected. I went ahead because the user's words, as you quoted them, name §explicit-token and override it. But they reached me through you, not from the user directly. Please make sure that quote is really the user's before this is committed.
- **Record layout:** I kept the four house fields and put the override note and incident history in `Reason`, rather than adding a new field. I added an `- Evidence: \`token.test.mjs\`` line, as `src/tabs/PRECEDENT.md` does, because the record now requires test isolation. I dropped the old "keeps the token's origin visible at the call site" sentence, since it argued against the fallback and no longer applies. The incident is kept.
- **Hook asked for a test-file section:** the hook said to add a `## token.test.mjs` section for decisions about that file. Your brief asked for one block, rewritten in place, so I put the isolation rule there instead.
- **Top comment:** unchanged, because "Resolves the API token for a client instance." is still accurate.
- **Test split:** the `resolveToken()` and `resolveToken({})` fallback cases are two separate tests, so there are 6 auth tests.
- **Empty or non-string token falls back:** `{ token: "" }`, or a token that isn't a string, now falls back to `API_TOKEN` instead of throwing. That follows "explicit non-empty string wins", but passing `""` can't be used to opt out of the env. No test covers it; say if you want one.
- **Hook scope without process isolation:** the save/restore hooks are at the top of the file. Under `node --test --test-isolation=none`, Node applies them to every test file in the process, so other files' tests would also run with `API_TOKEN` cleared and restored. That's harmless, and plain `npm test` runs each file in its own process. Wrapping the tests in `describe` would scope the hooks if that matters.

